<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ottokar IV of Styria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1163">1163</a>–<a href="/years/1192">1192</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ottokar IV was the last of the Otakar rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> and its first duke, raised to that rank by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> in 1180. Childless and ill, he concluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgenberg-pact">Georgenberg Pact</a> in 1186, leaving Styria to the Babenberg dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, who inherited on his death in 1192.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ottokar-iv-of-styria.jpg" alt="Ottokar IV of Styria" width="320" height="313" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Ottokar_IV_of_Styria_(127907085).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ottokar was the only son of Margrave Ottokar III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> and his wife Kunigunde, a daughter of the Bavarian margrave Diepold III of Vohburg and sister-in-law of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>. His father, who died on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> in 1164, left him extensive estates stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> down the Mur river to the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>. By the mid 12th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otakars">Otakars</a> had moved their residence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steyr">Steyr</a> to the Castle Hill, the Schlossberg, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a>. Still a minor, Ottokar was raised under the tutelage of his mother and the Styrian ministeriales.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As margrave from 1164, Ottokar came into conflict with the neighbouring Babenberg dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and with the Spanheim duke Herman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>. Backed by his uncle the emperor, he worked to secure the imperial border against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> in the east, rebuilding his residence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a> and raising the fortress of Fürstenfeld about 1170. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a> of Gelnhausen in 1180 declared the rebellious Bavarian duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> deposed, the emperor detached the Styrian march from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and made it an independent duchy.</p><p>Ottokar had contracted leprosy on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>. With the emperor's consent he met Duke Leopold V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enns-river">Enns</a>, where they agreed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> would pass to Leopold and his son Frederick on condition that Austria and Styria remain undivided. He was buried in his father's foundation of Žiče Charterhouse, and in 1827 his remains were moved to Rein Abbey. His gravestone of around 1200, showing him at full length, is now in the church of Sveta Areha in Frajhajm, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ottokar IV of Styria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Georgenberg near Enns, the childless Duke <b>Ottokar IV of Styria</b> agreed that his duchy would pass to Duke Leopold V of Austria and the Babenbergs, guaranteeing the rights of the Styrian nobles and church.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-17">17 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1180 Emperor Frederick Barbarossa raised it to a duchy for <b>Ottokar IV</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-styria">Duchy of Styria</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1180 Frederick Barbarossa raised Styria to a duchy under <b>Ottokar IV</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-styria">Margraviate of Styria</a></li></ul></section>
